/-! # A principal complete normal counterexample to quasi-excellence lifting

Source: Kurano–Shimomoto, Ideal-adic completion of quasi-excellent rings (after Gabber),
Theorem 5.1, Step 1 (1-1)–(1-4), pp. 7–8; (5.3) and Step 3, pp. 9–12.
(blueprint: lem-per-gabber-normal-counterexample-reduction)
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

/-- A counterexample to quasi-excellence lifting reduces to a principal complete normal
Nagata domain, with the stated properties of all finite normalizations.

Source: Kurano–Shimomoto, Theorem 5.1, Step 1 (1-1)–(1-4), pp. 7–8, (5.3), p. 9,
and Step 3. No characteristic assumption is made.
(blueprint: lem-per-gabber-normal-counterexample-reduction)

Proof plan (the eight steps of the node):
1. Write $I=(a_1,\ldots,a_t)$ and $I_i=(a_1,\ldots,a_i)$. Each $A/I_i$ is complete for the image
   of $I$, since it is a finite $A$-module, and hence for the subideal generated by the image of
   $a_{i+1}$. The sequence begins with a non-quasi-excellent ring and ends with a quasi-excellent
   ring, so choose adjacent terms with the latter property first appearing. Put $C=A/I_i$ and
   $x=a_{i+1}\bmod I_i$. Then $C$ is $(x)$-complete, not quasi-excellent, and $C/xC$ is
   quasi-excellent. Here $x\ne0$, since otherwise the adjacent terms coincide; $x$ is a nonunit
   since $(x)$ lies in the Jacobson radical of the nonzero complete ring $C$. Quotients remain
   Noetherian: generators of the inverse image ideal descend to generators of the quotient ideal.
   If $I=0$, the hypotheses already contradict one another, so the adjacent transition exists with
   $t>0$.
   Uses: def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring,
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-module-complete-over-complete-noetherian, lem-sfb-adic-complete-subideal,
   lem-weil-pbc-adic-complete-jacobson-radical.
2. Among the ideals $J\subset C$ with $C/J$ not quasi-excellent choose a maximal one, using the
   maximal condition for submodules of the finite $C$-module $C$. Put $D=C/J$. It is Noetherian,
   $(x)$-complete, and not quasi-excellent. Every nonzero ideal $H\subset D$ pulls back to an
   ideal strictly containing $J$, so $D/H$ is quasi-excellent. Moreover $D/xD=C/(J+xC)$ is a
   quotient of $C/xC$, hence quasi-excellent by finite-type stability on spectra, applied to
   $\operatorname{Spec}(C/(J+xC))\to\operatorname{Spec}(C/xC)$. Its hypothesis that every affine
   open of $Y=\operatorname{Spec}(C/xC)$ has quasi-excellent ring holds: $Y$ is locally Noetherian
   by its one-chart cover [pqf-def-locnoeth], and the cover-locality lemmas
   [lem-trf-g-ring-affine-cover, lem-trf-j2-affine-cover] applied to that cover give the G-ring
   and J-2 properties on every affine open, which is Noetherian as a G-ring. The same check
   applies to every affine base below. Thus $x\ne0$ in $D$, and completeness again makes it a
   nonunit.
   Uses: lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-finite-module-acc,
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-module-complete-over-complete-noetherian,
   lem-per-quasi-excellent-finite-type-stability, lem-weil-pbc-adic-complete-jacobson-radical,
   pqf-def-locnoeth, lem-trf-g-ring-affine-cover, lem-trf-j2-affine-cover.
3. The complete ideal $(x)$ lies in every maximal ideal of $D$. If $D$ were a G-ring, the
   Jacobson-quotient criterion would make $D$ quasi-excellent, a contradiction. The pair-of-primes
   criterion therefore supplies primes $Q\subseteq P$ whose quotient generic formal fibre is not
   geometrically regular. If $Q\ne0$, then $D/Q$ is quasi-excellent by the preceding maximality,
   so is a G-ring and that same criterion makes this fibre geometrically regular, a contradiction.
   Hence $Q=0$ is prime, so $D$ is a domain.
   Uses: lem-weil-pbc-adic-complete-jacobson-radical, lem-per-qe-jacobson-g-ring-criterion,
   lem-weil-pbc-g-ring-check-pairs-of-primes, def-exc-excellent-ring.
4. Since $D/xD$ is quasi-excellent it is Nagata, and Nagata lifting makes $D$ Nagata. Let $R$ be
   its integral closure in $\operatorname{Frac}D$. The N-2 property, applied to the identity
   extension of fraction fields, makes $R$ finite over $D$, and the integral-closure lemma makes
   it a normal domain. It is Noetherian and Nagata by finite-algebra permanence, and
   $(x)$-complete by finite-module completeness. Lying over makes
   $\operatorname{Spec}R\to\operatorname{Spec}D$ surjective. If $R$ were quasi-excellent,
   finite-surjective descent would make $D$ quasi-excellent. Thus $R$ is not quasi-excellent. The
   injection $D\subset R$ preserves $x\ne0$, and completeness makes $x$ a nonunit.
   Uses: lem-exc-quasi-excellent-nagata, lem-per-adic-nagata-lifting,
   lem-weil-pbc-nagata-domain-n2, def-weil-pbc-japanese-domain,
   lem-weil-pbc-integral-closure-finite-extension-normal, lem-weil-pbc-finite-over-nagata-nagata,
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-module-complete-over-complete-noetherian,
   lem-weil-pbc-integral-injective-lying-over, lem-per-qe-finite-surjective-descent,
   lem-weil-pbc-adic-complete-jacobson-radical.
5. For a nonzero ideal $K\subset R$ choose $0\ne b\in K$. As $R\subset\operatorname{Frac}D$, write
   $b=a/s$ with $a,s\in D$, $s\ne0$. Then $a=sb\ne0$ belongs to $K\cap D$. Thus $H=K\cap D\ne0$
   and $D/H$ is quasi-excellent. The ring $R/K$ is a finite algebra over $D/H$ (images of the
   finite $D$-module generators of $R$ generate it), so it is quasi-excellent by finite-type
   stability. This includes $K=R$: the zero ring is Noetherian, its prime spectrum is empty, and
   both G-ring and J-2 conditions hold (its unital algebras are zero). In particular $R/xR$ is
   quasi-excellent.
   Uses: lem-per-quasi-excellent-finite-type-stability.
6. Fix any finite extension $L/\operatorname{Frac}R$ and let $B_L$ be the integral closure. Since
   $R$ is Nagata its N-2 property makes $B_L$ finite over $R$. The integral-closure lemma makes
   $B_L$ a normal domain with fraction field $L$. Finite-algebra permanence makes it Noetherian
   and Nagata, and finite-module completeness makes it $(x)$-complete. The quotient $B_L/xB_L$ is
   a finite algebra over $R/xR$ and is therefore quasi-excellent. Apply normal-completion lifting
   to $B_L$ with ideal $xB_L$: for every prime $\mathfrak p$ of $B_L$, the completion of
   $(B_L)_{\mathfrak p}$ is a normal local domain. This supplies, in particular, all the requested
   maximal-ideal completions.
   Uses: lem-weil-pbc-nagata-domain-n2, def-weil-pbc-japanese-domain,
   lem-weil-pbc-integral-closure-finite-extension-normal, lem-weil-pbc-finite-over-nagata-nagata,
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-module-complete-over-complete-noetherian,
   lem-per-quasi-excellent-finite-type-stability, lem-per-adic-normal-completion-lifting.
7. Apply the preceding argument also with $L=\operatorname{Frac}R$, for which $B_L=R$ by
   normality. For $T=R$ or any $B_L$ and any prime $\mathfrak p\subset T$, the localization
   $T_{\mathfrak p}$ is a Noetherian domain, and its completion is a domain, hence has a single
   irreducible component; its Krull dimension equals $\dim T_{\mathfrak p}$ and is finite by
   [lem-weil-pbc-completion-dimension], so the spectrum is equidimensional. The sole minimal prime
   of $T_{\mathfrak p}$ is zero, so its extension to the completion is zero as well. The Ratliff
   criterion therefore makes every $T_{\mathfrak p}$ universally catenary.
   Uses: def-weil-pbc-normal-ring, lem-weil-pbc-localization-noetherian,
   lem-weil-pbc-localization-normal-domain, lem-per-ratliff-formal-catenarity,
   lem-weil-pbc-completion-dimension.
8. This implies universal catenarity of $T$. Indeed, in a finite-type $T$-algebra $E$, take primes
   $\mathfrak a\subseteq\mathfrak b$ and put $\mathfrak p=\mathfrak b\cap T$. Every prime in the
   interval $[\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b]$ avoids $T\setminus\mathfrak p$. Localizing at this set
   therefore gives an order isomorphism of the entire prime interval with its image in the
   finite-type $T_{\mathfrak p}$-algebra $E\otimes_TT_{\mathfrak p}$. The inverse sends a
   localized prime to its contraction: for a prime avoiding this set, $a/s$ is in its extension
   exactly when $a$ is in the prime, since the denominator and every multiplier from the set avoid
   the prime. These inverse inclusion-preserving maps preserve lengths and saturation of chains.
   Catenarity of that algebra supplies both the finite bound and equality of saturated lengths in
   the original interval. Thus $T$ is universally catenary, by its definition, for both $R$ and
   every $B_L$. All required properties now hold.
   Uses: def-exc-excellent-ring, def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime.
-/
theorem exists_gabber_normal_counterexample
    (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A]
    (I : Ideal A) [IsAdicComplete I A]
    (hquot : IsQuasiExcellent (A ⧸ I)) (hA : ¬ IsQuasiExcellent A) :
    ∃ (R : Type u) (_ : CommRing R) (_ : IsDomain R) (_ : IsNoetherianRing R),
      IsIntegrallyClosed R ∧ IsNagataRing R ∧
      ∃ x : R, x ≠ 0 ∧ ¬ IsUnit x ∧
        IsAdicComplete (Ideal.span {x}) R ∧ IsUniversallyCatenary R ∧
        ¬ IsQuasiExcellent R ∧
        (∀ K : Ideal R, K ≠ ⊥ → IsQuasiExcellent (R ⧸ K)) ∧
        ∀ (L : Type (max u v)) [Field L] [Algebra (FractionRing R) L]
          [Algebra R L] [IsScalarTower R (FractionRing R) L]
          [FiniteDimensional (FractionRing R) L],
          Module.Finite R (integralClosure R L) ∧
          IsIntegrallyClosed (integralClosure R L) ∧
          IsNagataRing (integralClosure R L) ∧
          IsUniversallyCatenary (integralClosure R L) ∧
          IsAdicComplete
            ((Ideal.span {x}).map (algebraMap R (integralClosure R L)))
            (integralClosure R L) ∧
          IsQuasiExcellent ((integralClosure R L) ⧸
            (Ideal.span {x}).map (algebraMap R (integralClosure R L))) ∧
          ∀ (n : Ideal (integralClosure R L)) [n.IsMaximal],
            IsLocalRing (AdicCompletion
              (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (Localization.AtPrime n)) (Localization.AtPrime n)) ∧
            IsDomain (AdicCompletion
              (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (Localization.AtPrime n)) (Localization.AtPrime n)) ∧
            IsIntegrallyClosed (AdicCompletion
              (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (Localization.AtPrime n)) (Localization.AtPrime n)) := by
  sorry
